/-!
# Divisorial charts of regular strict normal crossings pairs

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Exposé VI 1.2--1.5(i), printed pp. 69--70;
IX 2.1, printed p. 133; VIII Remark 4.2.2(i), printed p. 118
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-boundary-divisorial-chart).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A regular Noetherian strict normal crossings pair has its canonical divisorial
fine saturated log structure and pointed free charts by boundary parameters.
Source: ILO VI 1.2--1.5(i), VIII Remark 4.2.2(i), IX 2.1, pp. 69--70, 118, 133
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-boundary-divisorial-chart).

Proof:
1. At an ordinary point, each boundary component has regular codimension-one quotient.
   The regular-quotient kernel parameter theorem and regular-sequence dimension formula
   show that its ideal is generated by one parameter. Choose these generators separately.
2. Represent the finitely many germs on one affine neighbourhood. Clear finitely many
   denominators to identify the component ideals with their principal equations there.
   Remove the other components. The complement is the principal open of their product;
   the product is invertible at every generic point, so the complement is dense.
3. At every point of this neighbourhood, the intersection criterion gives a regular
   quotient of codimension equal to the number of vanishing component equations. The
   cotangent dimension calculation makes their classes independent. Extend to a basis
   and apply Nakayama: these actual equations extend to a regular parameter system.
4. Compare geometric strict local rings with strict henselizations using
   lem-exc-affine-strict-local-ring-comparison. Strict-henselization regularity and the
   cotangent comparison preserve that parameter system and its dimension. The quotient
   by its boundary prefix is regular with the required dimension.
5. The prescribed divisorial sections form an étale sheaf and satisfy the log unit
   condition. Their stalk consists of the elements becoming units after inverting the
   boundary product: an inverse is witnessed by one finite equation f g = product^n,
   which descends to a pointed neighbourhood. Equality descends in the same way.
6. Apply lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization. Every such germ is a unit
   times a monomial in the vanishing boundary parameters. Cotangent independence makes
   those primes pairwise nonassociate, so both the exponents and the unit are unique.
7. The free monoid chart on the neighbourhood is a chart at every geometric point:
   coordinates which become units are absorbed into the unit factor of the pushout;
   the remaining coordinates and units give precisely the factorization in Step 6.
   At the selected centre all coordinates remain nonunits, so the induced map onto the
   characteristic is an isomorphism. The affine open is a pointed étale neighbourhood.
8. The free monoids are fine saturated. Their characteristic vanishes exactly off the
   boundary. A nonunit monomial is divisible by a boundary parameter, so the nonunit
   ideal is exactly their generated ideal. Step 4 supplies regularity and the dimension
   formula; the characteristic group has rank r. This is log regularity. For the empty
   boundary r = 0, the log structure is the units, its nonunit ideal is zero, and the
   dimension formula is the identity. No field base or excellence is used.
-/
theorem regularBoundaryDivisorialChart
    (Y : Scheme.{u}) [IsNoetherian Y] (_hY : IsRegularScheme Y)
    (T : Y.IdealSheafData) (_hT : IsStrictNormalCrossingsBoundary T) :
    ∃ hV : DenseRange (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).ι,
      IsDivisorialFineSaturatedLogRegularPair Y
        (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).toScheme
        (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).ι hV ∧
      ∃ (M : EtaleLogStructure Y)
        (e : M.monoidSheaf.obj ≅ divisorialLogPresheaf (V := Y)
          (U := (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).toScheme)
          (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).ι),
        NatTrans.vcomp e.hom (divisorialStructuralMap (V := Y)
          (U := (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).toScheme)
          (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).ι) = M.alpha ∧
        M.IsFineSaturated ∧
        M.trivialityLocus =
          Set.range (Scheme.openComplementOfIsClosedImmersion T.subschemeι).ι ∧
        ∀ (Ω : Type u) [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω] (y : Spec (.of Ω) ⟶ Y),
          ∃ (r : ℕ) (W : Y.Etale) (w : (Scheme.pointSmallEtale y).fiber.obj W)
            (β : ULift.{u} (Multiplicative (Fin r → ℕ)) →*
              M.monoidSheaf.obj.obj (op W)),
            M.IsChart W β ∧
            Function.Bijective (fun p : ULift.{u} (Multiplicative (Fin r → ℕ)) ↦
              Associates.mk (M.toEtalePrelogStructure.germ y W w (β p))) ∧
            ∃ (hA : IsRegularLocalRing (strictLocalRing Y y))
              (t : Fin r → strictLocalRing Y y),
              (∀ i : Fin r,
                M.toEtalePrelogStructure.stalkAlpha y
                  (M.toEtalePrelogStructure.germ y W w
                    (β (ULift.up (Multiplicative.ofAdd
                      (fun q : Fin r ↦ if q = i then 1 else 0))))) = t i) ∧
              (∃ s : List (strictLocalRing Y y),
                @IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (strictLocalRing Y y) _
                  hA.toIsLocalRing hA.toIsNoetherian (List.ofFn t ++ s)) ∧
              M.nonunitIdeal y = Ideal.span (Set.range t) ∧
              IsRegularRing (strictLocalRing Y y ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range t)) ∧
              ringKrullDim (strictLocalRing Y y) =
                ringKrullDim (strictLocalRing Y y ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range t)) +
                  (r : WithBot ℕ∞) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
